1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,344
An engineer is asked which files use the
tenant field before a schema change.

2
00:00:09,384 --> 00:00:14,418
She runs git grep in her clone, gets no
match, and reports that nothing uses it.

3
00:00:14,458 --> 00:00:17,653
Three files use it. The
change breaks two services.

4
00:00:17,993 --> 00:00:21,415
Her repository wasn't damaged,
and her command wasn't wrong.

5
00:00:21,455 --> 00:00:26,204
Her clone was a sparse checkout, a working
tree that holds only chosen directories.

6
00:00:26,244 --> 00:00:30,358
She asked a working-tree question
where a repository question was meant.

7
00:00:30,698 --> 00:00:35,580
Later the same week she narrows her cone,
the set of directories she chose, and

8
00:00:35,620 --> 00:00:39,380
loses a downloaded evaluation
set that took an hour to fetch.

9
00:00:39,420 --> 00:00:44,551
Git printed nothing and exited with status
0. Both events follow from one rule, and

10
00:00:44,591 --> 00:00:47,728
that rule comes first. Hold
on to that silent exit.

11
00:00:49,568 --> 00:00:53,299
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:53,339 --> 00:00:58,183
Here's the rule, from section 24.5: a
path with the skip-worktree bit is in the

13
00:00:58,223 --> 00:01:01,768
index and in history, and Git
does not look at it on disk.

14
00:01:01,808 --> 00:01:06,961
The index is Git's record of every tracked
path, and the skip-worktree bit is a flag

15
00:01:07,001 --> 00:01:08,321
on one of its entries.

16
00:01:08,661 --> 00:01:11,781
In the last video you set a
cone. Now you live in one.

17
00:01:11,821 --> 00:01:16,580
You'll see which commands see the cone and
which see everything, what git add does

18
00:01:16,620 --> 00:01:21,497
outside the cone, and what happens to
untracked and ignored files when the cone gets

19
00:01:21,537 --> 00:01:26,120
narrower. Then the sparse index: a way
to make the index itself shrink with the

20
00:01:26,160 --> 00:01:26,413
cone.

21
00:01:26,753 --> 00:01:37,085
Two replays: labs/ch24/sparse-behaviour.sh
and labs/ch24/sparse-index.sh.

22
00:01:37,425 --> 00:01:42,680
Labels. git grep is 🟢 SAFE. git sparse-checkout
reapply and set --sparse-index are 🟡

23
00:01:42,720 --> 00:01:47,660
CAUTION. git sparse-checkout clean is 🔴
DANGEROUS, and it gets its five answers

24
00:01:47,700 --> 00:01:48,606
before it runs.

25
00:01:50,446 --> 00:01:51,858
After this video you can:

26
00:01:52,198 --> 00:01:55,679
Predict what search, status and
add do for paths outside the cone.

27
00:01:56,019 --> 00:02:00,185
Explain what happens to untracked and
ignored files when a cone is narrowed.

28
00:02:00,525 --> 00:02:04,061
Clean leftovers after narrowing
a cone, with a dry run first.

29
00:02:04,401 --> 00:02:09,724
Say what the sparse index changes, why only
in cone mode, and what makes a command

30
00:02:09,764 --> 00:02:10,379
expand it.

31
00:02:12,219 --> 00:02:14,508
Start from the rule and
derive the behaviour.

32
00:02:14,848 --> 00:02:19,827
Commands that read commits, trees and blobs
see the whole repository: git log on a

33
00:02:19,867 --> 00:02:23,439
path outside the cone, git show
of a file outside the cone.

34
00:02:23,479 --> 00:02:28,274
Commands that read the working tree, the
files on your disk, see only the cone.

35
00:02:28,314 --> 00:02:31,396
git grep without options
searches the working tree.

36
00:02:31,436 --> 00:02:35,741
It finds nothing in files that aren't
there, and exits with "no match".

37
00:02:35,781 --> 00:02:40,883
That's a wrong answer without a warning.
git grep --cached searches the index, which

38
00:02:40,923 --> 00:02:41,801
has every path.

39
00:02:42,141 --> 00:02:45,991
Why accept that? Because the cone
pays off in work per command.

40
00:02:46,031 --> 00:02:51,116
git status asks the file system only about
files inside the cone and walks only the

41
00:02:51,156 --> 00:02:52,536
directories that exist.

42
00:02:52,876 --> 00:02:57,536
Now files outside the cone. A working
tree gets them in two everyday ways.

43
00:02:57,576 --> 00:03:02,618
The first: you or a tool create one. git
add then refuses with exit status 1 and

44
00:03:02,658 --> 00:03:07,763
names the ways forward. With git add
--sparse the file is staged, and it stays on

45
00:03:07,803 --> 00:03:12,972
disk until git sparse-checkout reapply
removes it. The second way, in the manual's

46
00:03:13,012 --> 00:03:18,118
description of reapply: a merge or rebase
may "materialize paths to do their work

47
00:03:18,158 --> 00:03:23,009
(e.g. in order to show you a conflict)".
The same reapply cleans up after the

48
00:03:23,049 --> 00:03:23,708
resolution.

49
00:03:24,048 --> 00:03:27,189
Then narrowing. Narrowing a
cone removes tracked files.

50
00:03:27,229 --> 00:03:31,239
That's safe: they're in the index
and in history. What about the rest?

51
00:03:31,579 --> 00:03:35,812
Untracked files have no index
entry and match no ignore pattern.

52
00:03:35,852 --> 00:03:39,218
Git keeps such a file and
its directory, and warns.

53
00:03:39,258 --> 00:03:44,693
Removing such directories is the job of
git sparse-checkout clean, which needs Git

54
00:03:44,733 --> 00:03:45,628
2.52 or later.

55
00:03:45,968 --> 00:03:50,543
Ignored files, the ones an ignore
pattern matches, get no warning.

56
00:03:50,583 --> 00:03:56,347
The manual states the rule: if everything
untracked in a directory outside the cone

57
00:03:56,387 --> 00:04:01,662
is ignored, "then the directory will be
deleted". For a build cache that's a

58
00:04:01,702 --> 00:04:07,256
convenience. For an ignored file you can't
regenerate, such as a local .env or a

59
00:04:07,296 --> 00:04:13,130
downloaded evaluation set, narrowing the
cone is a 🔴 operation whose only preview is

60
00:04:13,170 --> 00:04:15,158
git status --short --ignored.

61
00:04:15,498 --> 00:04:20,600
Now the sparse index. The problem it solves:
after everything above, each command

62
00:04:20,640 --> 00:04:25,616
still reads, and often rewrites, an index
proportional to the whole repository.

63
00:04:25,656 --> 00:04:30,949
In one sentence: a sparse index replaces
all entries of a directory outside the cone

64
00:04:30,989 --> 00:04:36,092
by one entry that names the directory's
tree, so the index shrinks with the cone.

65
00:04:36,132 --> 00:04:39,520
A tree is the object that
holds one directory listing.

66
00:04:39,860 --> 00:04:44,760
Now precisely. With index.sparse=true,
written by git sparse-checkout set

67
00:04:44,800 --> 00:04:50,445
--sparse-index, the index may hold sparse
directory entries: mode 040000, a tree ID,

68
00:04:50,485 --> 00:04:53,963
the skip-worktree bit, and
a path ending in a slash.

69
00:04:54,003 --> 00:04:59,310
It needs cone mode, because only cone
mode guarantees that a whole directory is

70
00:04:59,350 --> 00:05:04,182
either in or out. The manual marks it
separately: "This feature is still

71
00:05:04,222 --> 00:05:09,663
experimental", and it warns that external
tools may not understand such an index.

72
00:05:10,003 --> 00:05:14,844
The cost: a command that needs paths inside
a collapsed directory expands the index

73
00:05:14,884 --> 00:05:18,489
in memory first, and Git itself
calls that "a slow operation".

74
00:05:18,829 --> 00:05:23,718
When should you use the sparse index? When
the index itself is the bottleneck, which

75
00:05:23,758 --> 00:05:28,354
means hundreds of thousands of tracked
files, and when the tools that share the

76
00:05:28,394 --> 00:05:33,165
working tree cope with it: the editor
integration, the prompt, the hook framework,

77
00:05:33,205 --> 00:05:37,860
an older Git. When not? When a tool runs
an expanding command on every prompt or

78
00:05:37,900 --> 00:05:39,679
save. That cancels the benefit.

79
00:05:41,519 --> 00:05:46,168
The textbook's analogy for the sparse
index: a table of contents that lists every

80
00:05:46,208 --> 00:05:50,162
section of the chapters you read and
one line for each other chapter.

81
00:05:50,202 --> 00:05:54,909
Whoever needs a section of a collapsed
chapter must expand the line first, and the

82
00:05:54,949 --> 00:05:56,472
expansion is the slow part.

83
00:05:56,812 --> 00:06:00,587
The analogy breaks at who decides.
A reader chooses to expand.

84
00:06:00,627 --> 00:06:05,387
Git expands whenever a command asks for
a path that the collapsed entry cannot

85
00:06:05,427 --> 00:06:05,817
answer.

86
00:06:06,157 --> 00:06:11,114
For the first half of the video, keep the
question you learned to ask in the last

87
00:06:11,154 --> 00:06:14,568
video: which of the three
places does this command read?

88
00:06:14,608 --> 00:06:19,564
HEAD and history, the index, or the disk.
If it reads the disk, it sees the cone.

89
00:06:19,904 --> 00:06:24,664
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper.
Write those three places down.

90
00:06:24,704 --> 00:06:30,383
Next to each, put the one that reads it:
git log, git grep, or git grep --cached.

91
00:06:30,423 --> 00:06:31,089
I'll wait.

92
00:06:35,929 --> 00:06:40,077
git log reads history. git
grep --cached reads the index.

93
00:06:40,117 --> 00:06:44,192
Plain git grep reads the disk,
so it sees only the cone.

94
00:06:46,032 --> 00:06:51,072
On the left, the index you know: 33
entries, most of them marked S, for

95
00:06:51,112 --> 00:06:56,726
skip-worktree. On the right, the same
content with index.sparse on: 14 entries.

96
00:06:56,766 --> 00:07:02,307
Each directory that lies wholly outside
the cone has become one line with mode

97
00:07:02,347 --> 00:07:03,810
040000 and a tree ID.

98
00:07:04,150 --> 00:07:08,290
Look at services. It isn't collapsed,
because the cone reaches into it.

99
00:07:08,330 --> 00:07:13,234
Its two outside subdirectories collapse
separately, and the four files of the ranker

100
00:07:13,274 --> 00:07:14,471
stay as file entries.

101
00:07:14,811 --> 00:07:19,232
Read the root-cause line: a working-tree
question was asked where a repository

102
00:07:19,272 --> 00:07:21,634
question was meant. The fix is to ask Git.

103
00:07:23,474 --> 00:07:26,792
The cone is services/ranker
and libs/tokenizer.

104
00:07:27,132 --> 00:07:30,694
The gateway is outside the cone. Which of
these four commands give an answer about

105
00:07:30,734 --> 00:07:32,056
it? Say it out loud. I'll wait.

106
00:07:36,896 --> 00:07:41,035
git log and git show answer:
history and objects are complete.

107
00:07:41,075 --> 00:07:45,887
git grep finds nothing and exits 1.
git grep --cached finds three files.

108
00:07:45,927 --> 00:07:46,966
That's the hook.

109
00:07:47,306 --> 00:07:52,599
The work of one git status, counted. The
index still has 33 entries to read, but Git

110
00:07:52,639 --> 00:07:57,742
asked the file system about nine files
where a full checkout needs 33, and walked

111
00:07:57,782 --> 00:08:00,473
six directories where
it would walk twenty.

112
00:08:00,813 --> 00:08:02,446
A new file outside the cone.

113
00:08:02,786 --> 00:08:08,047
Quick quiz, two options. A plain git add
of a new file in a directory outside the

114
00:08:08,087 --> 00:08:10,730
cone: staged or refused? Say it out loud.

115
00:08:15,570 --> 00:08:19,483
Refused, with exit status 1,
and the hint names --sparse.

116
00:08:19,523 --> 00:08:24,478
If you said staged, that's a fair
guess: staging is what git add is for.

117
00:08:24,818 --> 00:08:29,146
The file is committed and still on
disk, an H entry among S entries.

118
00:08:29,186 --> 00:08:34,350
git sparse-checkout reapply, 🟡, enforces
the cone again: the file leaves the disk

119
00:08:34,390 --> 00:08:35,892
and its entry becomes S.

120
00:08:36,232 --> 00:08:37,492
Now untracked files.

121
00:08:37,832 --> 00:08:40,861
Git kept the untracked file
and its directory, and warned.

122
00:08:41,201 --> 00:08:46,852
Now the 🔴 command, git sparse-checkout
clean. Twig looks worried, and fairly so.

123
00:08:46,892 --> 00:08:50,551
The five answers. What it
changes: the working tree.

124
00:08:50,591 --> 00:08:56,314
What it can destroy: untracked files there,
here results.tmp, which were never in

125
00:08:56,354 --> 00:09:02,147
Git, so no reflog and no git fsck returns
them. Preview: --dry-run, with --verbose

126
00:09:02,187 --> 00:09:08,051
for every file. Recovery: none from Git.
Appropriate: after reading the preview and

127
00:09:08,091 --> 00:09:09,901
moving away what you need.

128
00:09:10,241 --> 00:09:14,915
The dry run says it would remove
the whole directory pipelines/.

129
00:09:14,955 --> 00:09:21,103
Without --force or --dry-run the command
refuses. With -f the directory is gone, the

130
00:09:21,143 --> 00:09:22,798
untracked file with it.

131
00:09:23,138 --> 00:09:27,724
The same sequence with an ignored file.
build/ is ignored in this repository.

132
00:09:28,064 --> 00:09:32,488
Last time Git warned and kept the
file. This time the file is ignored.

133
00:09:32,528 --> 00:09:36,250
What does narrowing print? Make
your prediction. I'll wait.

134
00:09:41,090 --> 00:09:47,133
Nothing. Exit status 0, and the cache is
gone. That's the silent exit from the hook.

135
00:09:49,973 --> 00:09:55,237
A sparse checkout with an ordinary
index: 33 entries for 7 files on disk.

136
00:10:00,777 --> 00:10:05,738
Fourteen entries. This is the right-hand
side of the diagram, with the real tree

137
00:10:05,778 --> 00:10:05,988
IDs.

138
00:10:06,328 --> 00:10:10,617
The entry for docs/ holds exactly
the ID of the docs tree in HEAD.

139
00:10:10,657 --> 00:10:14,947
So Git can compare such an entry
with a commit without opening it.

140
00:10:15,287 --> 00:10:19,633
Both counters now follow the cone:
14 entries read, 7 lstat calls.

141
00:10:19,673 --> 00:10:25,216
On orbit that saves nothing you can feel.
The textbook cites GitHub's engineers, who

142
00:10:25,256 --> 00:10:30,799
reported an index shrinking from about 180
megabytes to under 10 megabytes on a test

143
00:10:30,839 --> 00:10:34,388
repository of two million
files. The figure is theirs.

144
00:10:34,728 --> 00:10:38,211
git ls-files without --sparse
must list every path.

145
00:10:38,251 --> 00:10:41,528
What does Git have to do
first? Say it out loud.

146
00:10:46,368 --> 00:10:50,544
It expands the index in memory
and says so: "a slow operation".

147
00:10:50,584 --> 00:10:55,765
The hint's guess at the cause is wrong
here. The file on disk stays sparse: 14

148
00:10:55,805 --> 00:10:56,301
entries.

149
00:10:56,641 --> 00:11:00,731
And the way back: git sparse-checkout
set --no-sparse-index.

150
00:11:02,571 --> 00:11:04,219
Five mistakes to watch for.

151
00:11:04,559 --> 00:11:09,532
Searching a sparse checkout with plain git
grep or a directory listing and trusting

152
00:11:09,572 --> 00:11:14,121
"no match". Root cause: a working-tree
question was asked where a repository

153
00:11:14,161 --> 00:11:18,348
question was meant; git grep --cached
and git ls-files read the index.

154
00:11:18,688 --> 00:11:23,033
Narrowing a cone over a directory with
ignored files that cannot be regenerated.

155
00:11:23,073 --> 00:11:27,582
Root cause: if everything untracked in a
directory outside the cone is ignored, the

156
00:11:27,622 --> 00:11:29,720
directory is deleted without a warning.

157
00:11:30,060 --> 00:11:33,444
Running git sparse-checkout
clean -f without the dry run.

158
00:11:33,484 --> 00:11:38,431
Root cause: it removes whole directories,
including untracked files that were never

159
00:11:38,471 --> 00:11:38,851
in Git.

160
00:11:39,191 --> 00:11:42,938
Expecting git add to stage a
new file outside the cone.

161
00:11:42,978 --> 00:11:48,722
Root cause: Git does not update index entries
outside the sparse-checkout definition

162
00:11:48,762 --> 00:11:50,443
unless --sparse is given.

163
00:11:50,783 --> 00:11:56,068
Enabling the sparse index and seeing no
gain. Root cause: a tool that asks for every

164
00:11:56,108 --> 00:11:58,921
path makes Git expand
the index on each call.

165
00:12:00,761 --> 00:12:05,598
Now, out of the lab. A search team's
pre-merge script lists Python files with find

166
00:12:05,638 --> 00:12:10,594
and checks that each has a test. In one
engineer's clone it reports that most of the

167
00:12:10,634 --> 00:12:14,698
repository has no tests, and in CI,
the automated checks, it's right.

168
00:12:14,738 --> 00:12:19,516
Her clone is a sparse checkout: git status
prints "You are in a sparse checkout".

169
00:12:19,556 --> 00:12:24,512
The script is changed to read from Git, with
git ls-files, so that it sees all files

170
00:12:24,552 --> 00:12:25,404
in every clone.

171
00:12:25,744 --> 00:12:31,694
The same team adopts a rule for narrowing
a cone: run git status --short --ignored

172
00:12:31,734 --> 00:12:37,611
first, and move away any ignored file that
cannot be regenerated, such as a local

173
00:12:37,651 --> 00:12:43,455
.env or a downloaded evaluation set. Then
narrow, then git sparse-checkout clean

174
00:12:43,495 --> 00:12:45,062
--dry-run, then clean.

175
00:12:46,902 --> 00:12:51,364
Your turn. Do Exercise 18.8, "A
file that is not on disk", in

176
00:12:51,404 --> 00:12:57,121
exercises/m16-m18-internals.md. Before
you run anything, state for the file in

177
00:12:57,161 --> 00:13:01,328
question where it exists: on
disk, in the index, in HEAD.

178
00:13:01,368 --> 00:13:07,307
Then predict what each command of the
exercise will report, and only then run it.

179
00:13:07,647 --> 00:13:12,981
The challenge is Exercise 18.9, "The clone
that believes it is up to date", in the

180
00:13:13,021 --> 00:13:13,637
same file.

181
00:13:15,477 --> 00:13:18,655
Question 431 of the CTO question bank:

182
00:13:20,995 --> 00:13:25,830
Answer out loud. A strong answer separates
three kinds of file, tracked, untracked

183
00:13:25,870 --> 00:13:28,921
and ignored, and gives a
different outcome for each.

184
00:13:28,961 --> 00:13:33,914
It says which outcome comes with a warning
and which without, names the command that

185
00:13:33,954 --> 00:13:38,551
removes leftovers and its preview, and
names the only preview there is for the

186
00:13:38,591 --> 00:13:42,355
silent case. It states plainly
what can't be recovered from Git.

187
00:13:44,195 --> 00:13:45,122
Let's land this.

188
00:13:45,462 --> 00:13:47,122
You should now be able to say:

189
00:13:47,462 --> 00:13:51,972
A path with the skip-worktree bit is in
the index and in history, and Git does not

190
00:13:52,012 --> 00:13:53,026
look at it on disk.

191
00:13:53,366 --> 00:13:58,688
Working-tree commands see the cone; index
and history commands see everything; git

192
00:13:58,728 --> 00:13:59,996
grep needs --cached.

193
00:14:00,336 --> 00:14:04,907
Narrowing a cone keeps untracked files with
a warning and deletes directories whose

194
00:14:04,947 --> 00:14:07,296
untracked content is
all ignored, silently.

195
00:14:07,636 --> 00:14:12,916
git sparse-checkout clean removes leftovers
and cannot be undone; run it with

196
00:14:12,956 --> 00:14:14,021
--dry-run first.

197
00:14:14,361 --> 00:14:19,330
The sparse index stores one tree entry per
directory outside the cone, needs cone

198
00:14:19,370 --> 00:14:22,792
mode, and expands when a
command asks for a path inside.

199
00:14:24,632 --> 00:14:29,396
Read sections 24.5 and 24.6 of Chapter 24.

200
00:14:29,736 --> 00:14:34,570
Today you watched a correct command give a
wrong answer, and you know which place to

201
00:14:34,610 --> 00:14:39,270
ask instead. Practise the three places on
one file before the next video: partial

202
00:14:39,310 --> 00:14:43,911
clone plus sparse checkout. Until then,
look at the state first and type second.

203
00:14:43,951 --> 00:14:45,304
See you in the next one.
